      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the physical appearance and attire of witnesses like a professional suit versus a dark hoodie can significantly influence a jury's subconscious perception, often regardless of legal instructions to ignore it. Lawyers frequently warn juries to \"look beyond first impressions,\" but cognitive biases towards perceived professionalism often lead jurors to unconsciously favor testimony from well-dressed, middle-aged, white males over those who appear less formal or work clothes are associated with marginalized groups. This bias can create a credibility gap where the same factual testimony is accepted from one witness type but dismissed as unreliable from another.",
